"Legal Section (https://linuxcnc.org/docs/2.9/html/common/gpld-copyright.html)\nciteturn27view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://linuxcnc.org/docs/2.9/html/common/gpld-copyright.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 102\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCopyright, Fair Use, and Licensing Statements for SRD, Data, Software, and Technical Series Publications | NIST (https://www.nist.gov/open/copyright-fair-use-and-licensing-statements-srd-data-software-and-technical-series-publications)\nciteturn27view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nist.gov/open/copyright-fair-use-and-licensing-statements-srd-data-software-and-technical-series-publications\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 230\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNIST Special Publication XXX (https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ir/2015/NIST.IR.8091.pdf)\nciteturn27view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 17; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ir/2015/NIST.IR.8091.pdf\",\"pattern\":\"incomplete\"}); Total lines: 529\nL174@P6: make use of Whitney’s methodology augmented with a taxonomy of assembly operations\nL175@P6: with associated performance metrics. Whitney describes the basic factors in system\nL176@P6: design as:\nL177@P6: 1. Analyze the product and determine alternate assembly methods and sequences,\nL178@P6: including for subassemblies. Determine the assembly process requirements,\nL179@P6: flexibility requirements, and problematic assembly steps.\nL180@P6: 2. Select an assembly sequence.\nL181@P6: 3. Determine the required production capacity of the system.\nL182@P6: 4. List the feasible assembly techniques for each operation and estimate cost and\nL183@P6: time for each.\nL184@P6: 5. Select a set of equipment or people that can make the product at the required rate\nL185@P6: for a reasonable cost.\nL186@P6: 6. Conduct an economic analysis and proceed to a detailed system design.\nL187@P6: Whitney then provides guidance for all the above steps, while acknowledging that there\nL188@P6: are no approaches that will work in all cases. The underlying assumption is that it is\nL189@P6: indeed possible to manufacture the assembly for a reasonable cost but a significant\nL190@P6: amount of analysis, simulation, and approximation will be needed to determine the best\nL191@P6: approach. It is also expected that multiple iterations will be necessary, not just to\nL192@P6: converge on an initial system, but to improve the system over time after production\nL193@P6: begins.\nL194@P6: The role of a taxonomy of assembly operations and of the equipment that carries out\nL195@P6: those operations is to provide alternative techniques that can be combined to make the\nL196@P6: desired product. Such a taxonomy is the subject of an earlier paper, Shneier, et al. [11].\nL197@P6: The taxonomy provides a hierarchical structure that can be traversed to link different\nL198@P6: components of a manufacturing system together to achieve the required capabilities. It\nL199@P6: does not include a way of combining performance measures, nor does it resolve the\nL200@P6: problem of different measures being important at different levels of the hierarchy, but it\nL201@P6-7: does constrain which items can be considered at each stage and gives some idea of the 5\nL202@P7: costs incurred and capabilities added when a selection is made. This taxonomy needs to\nL203@P7: be augmented with assembly-relevant constraints to allow the performance of the\nL204@P7: combined system to be estimated.\nL205@P7: The performance criteria for an assembly system at the highest level are economic and\nL206@P7: include cost, product quality, flexibility, throughput, reliability, and ease of setup and use\nL207@P7: (and re-use) of the equipment. The criteria for individual subsystems include the same\nL208@P7: economic measures and, more importantly, characteristics that determine the suitability\nL209@P7: of the subsystem to contribute to making the product. Performance measures for a\nL210@P7: manipulator or a sensor must enable a decision to be made about whether or not it will\nL211@P7: contribute to a step in the assembly and, if so, what constraints it will impose on the\nL212@P7: solution and how adding it will affect the total system’s cost and performance. An\nL213@P7: incomplete list of the kinds of measures that are needed includes:\nL214@P7:  Tolerances (e.g., variability by which a manipulator reaches a commanded position,\nL215@P7: uncertainty in the reported output of a sensor)\nL216@P7:  Time needed for execution (e.g., a manipulator picking up a part, a sensor locating a\nL217@P7: part)\nL218@P7:  Variability (e.g., in time, position)\nL219@P7:  Weight (e.g., load capacity of a manipulator, constraints on capacity in different parts\nL220@P7: of its reachable volume)\nL221@P7:  Size (e.g., the footprint of a machine or the field of view of a sensor, the largest-sized\nL222@P7: object a robot can pick up)\nL223@P7:  Reachability of equipment (e.g., the working volume of a manipulator)\nL224@P7:  Accuracy of equipment (e.g., Is a robot capable of mating parts well enough to\nL225@P7: achieve the assembly? Can a sensor measure to the required accuracy to acquire a\nL226@P7: part?)\nL227@P7:  Speed of equipment (e.g., can the subsystem execute within the time allotted?)\nL228@P7:  Part feeding (e.g., does the equipment need precise feeding? Does a sensor need to be\nL229@P7: added to locate the parts?)\nL230@P7:  Required system capacity (e.g., can the system as configured achieve the necessary\nL231@P7: throughput?)\nL232@P7:  Uptime of equipment (is the system reliable enough to ensure that the required\nL233@P7: capacity will be achieved? If a subsystem fails, what is the impact on the system as a\nL234@P7: whole?)\nL235@P7:  Tool changes required (if tool changes are needed, can the order of operations be\nL236@P7: modified to minimize them?)\nL237@P7:  Testing and inspection (can testing and inspection be incorporated in other steps or is\nL238@P7: a separate subsystem needed and, if so, how many?)\nL239@P7:  Constraints—does adding this subsystem limit how other subsystems can be used\nL240@P7: (e.g., a manipulator requires a sensor to be able to locate a part to within a specific\nL241@P7-8: tolerance)6\nL242@P8: The taxonomy of subsystems must represent the information necessary for these\nL243@P8: measures to be computed for the given situation. This is the case not only for equipment\nL244@P8: such as manipulators, sensors, or transportation devices, but also for the software that\nL245@P8: controls those devices. Then, when step 5 of Whitney’s procedure is reached, it should be\nL246@P8: possible to compare the different options to be added for each step of the task and choose\nL247@P8: the best feasible alternative.\nL248@P8-9: Figure 1. The assembly taxonomy from Shneier, et al. [11].7\nL249@P9: Figure 1 shows the assembly taxonomy. For each action in the hierarchy, there may be\nL250@P9: several available pieces of equipment that can achieve the action. Each of them should\nL251@P9: have relevant parameters from the list above that characterizes its performance. The\nL252@P9: actions also have constraints that contain further parameters limiting the applicability of a\nL253@P9: given piece of equipment.\nL254@P9: 3.1. Example\nL255@P9: To illustrate the approach, we present a simple example. Suppose the goal is to build a\nL256@P9: gearbox out of the set of parts shown in Figure 2. Assume that there are two robots\nL257@P9: available for this task together with two cameras and a mobile delivery vehicle\nL258@P9: (Automated Guided Vehicle, or AGV). One of the robots has force sensing, while the\nL259@P9: other does not. A person is also available to work on the assembly. The goal is to build\nL260@P9: the gearbox from the components, which will be delivered in a kit. A kit is a collection of\nL261@P9: the necessary parts, in this case placed in a flat tray but in unknown positions within the\nL262@P9: tray. The tray may be delivered to the work area by the vehicle and from the kit tray to\nL263@P9: the assembly area by one of the robots. Either or both robots may be selected to build the\nL264@P9: gearbox, but they have different capabilities. Similarly, any of the sensors may be used to\nL265@P9: locate the parts and inspect the results, but they have different fields of view and\nL266@P9: resolutions.\nL267@P9: Figure 2. A set of parts to be assembled into a gearbox.\nL268@P9: Figure 3. A view of the gearbox with all parts except the\nL269@P9: top cover assembled.\nL270@P9: 1. Analyze the product and determine alternate assembly methods and sequences,\nL271@P9: including subassemblies. Determine the assembly process requirements,\nL272@P9: flexibility requirements, and problematic assembly steps.\nL273@P9: The assembly is challenging for several reasons. There are a number of small\nL274@P9: parts that may be difficult for a robot to pick up and manipulate; the gears fit\nL275@P9: tightly and require a strategy for meshing; they are also similar in size and color.\nL276@P9-10: Visual sensing may have trouble with the parts because internal features may be 8\nL277@P10: difficult to detect and there may not be enough contrast with the background. The\nL278@P10: base of the gearbox (the part on the right of Error! Reference source not found.)\nL279@P10: can move as other parts are attached to it unless it is held or fixtured. A tool is\nL280@P10: required to screw the top onto the base. The small parts must be removed from the\nL281@P10: bag before use.\nL282@P10: A good starting heuristic is to look for steps that can only be accomplished in one\nL283@P10: way. Several of the challenges must be addressed by having a person assist with\nL284@P10: the assembly. There seems no alternative to having the person remove the small\nL285@P10: parts from the bag and insert the pins into the assembly. The person will also have\nL286@P10: to insert the screws because the robots have no tools to accomplish this step.\nL287@P10: There also seems not to be a choice about using the robot with force sensing to\nL288@P10: insert the gears because of the tight fit and need for meshing. The vision system\nL289@P10: must be able to recognize the parts and locate them accurately enough to direct\nL290@P10: the manipulator to grasp them. It must also be able to confirm that each step of the\nL291@P10: assembly has been completed correctly before the system moves on to the next\nL292@P10: step. This may not be possible if the field of view of the sensor cannot cover both\nL293@P10: the location where the kit is delivered and the place where the assembly is carried\nL294@P10: out. Thus, two vision systems may be required. Given that the kit of parts is\nL295@P10: delivered by an AGV, two vision systems will be used.\nL296@P10: There are a few alternatives with respect to how to proceed with the assembly.\nL297@P10: One of the robots can fetch the parts while the other inserts them, or one of the\nL298@P10: robots can act as a fixture and hold the base while the other robot does all the\nL299@P10: other activities, or one robot can fetch a part and then hold the base while it is\nL300@P10: added. The person can insert the pins that act as axes for the two smaller gears\nL301@P10: before the robot inserts the large gear or afterwards or can insert each pin just\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNIST Special Publication XXX (https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ir/2015/NIST.IR.8091.pdf)\nciteturn27view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 17; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ir/2015/NIST.IR.8091.pdf\",\"pattern\":\"gear\"}); Total lines: 529\nL272@P9: flexibility requirements, and problematic assembly steps.\nL273@P9: The assembly is challenging for several reasons. There are a number of small\nL274@P9: parts that may be difficult for a robot to pick up and manipulate; the gears fit\nL275@P9: tightly and require a strategy for meshing; they are also similar in size and color.\nL276@P9-10: Visual sensing may have trouble with the parts because internal features may be 8\nL277@P10: difficult to detect and there may not be enough contrast with the background. The\nL278@P10: base of the gearbox (the part on the right of Error! Reference source not found.)\nL279@P10: can move as other parts are attached to it unless it is held or fixtured. A tool is\nL280@P10: required to screw the top onto the base. The small parts must be removed from the\nL281@P10: bag before use.\nL282@P10: A good starting heuristic is to look for steps that can only be accomplished in one\nL283@P10: way. Several of the challenges must be addressed by having a person assist with\nL284@P10: the assembly. There seems no alternative to having the person remove the small\nL285@P10: parts from the bag and insert the pins into the assembly. The person will also have\nL286@P10: to insert the screws because the robots have no tools to accomplish this step.\nL287@P10: There also seems not to be a choice about using the robot with force sensing to\nL288@P10: insert the gears because of the tight fit and need for meshing. The vision system\nL289@P10: must be able to recognize the parts and locate them accurately enough to direct\nL290@P10: the manipulator to grasp them. It must also be able to confirm that each step of the\nL291@P10: assembly has been completed correctly before the system moves on to the next\nL292@P10: step. This may not be possible if the field of view of the sensor cannot cover both\nL293@P10: the location where the kit is delivered and the place where the assembly is carried\nL294@P10: out. Thus, two vision systems may be required. Given that the kit of parts is\nL295@P10: delivered by an AGV, two vision systems will be used.\nL296@P10: There are a few alternatives with respect to how to proceed with the assembly.\nL297@P10: One of the robots can fetch the parts while the other inserts them, or one of the\nL298@P10: robots can act as a fixture and hold the base while the other robot does all the\nL299@P10: other activities, or one robot can fetch a part and then hold the base while it is\nL300@P10: added. The person can insert the pins that act as axes for the two smaller gears\nL301@P10: before the robot inserts the large gear or afterwards or can insert each pin just\nL302@P10: before the robot adds the corresponding gear.\nL303@P10: 2. Select an assembly sequence.\nL304@P10: The taxonomy in Shneier, et al. [11] defines a set of possible actions to be used in\nL305@P10: the assembly. Each of these actions is associated in the taxonomy with a set of\nL306@P10: performance measures that are used to help select suitable equipment to perform\nL307@P10: the action. The italicized categories in parentheses below are actions from the\nL308@P10: taxonomy. For all of these actions, a cost can be associated with the taxonomy,\nL309@P10: and a weighted combination can be used to combine the costs into the overall task\nL310@P10: cost.\nL311@P10: In this example, the assembly sequence starts with the delivery of the kit\nL312@P10: (Transport). Then, the base of the gearbox must be picked up (Pick Up) and\nL313@P10: placed (Place) in the work area of one of the robots. This is done with visual input\nL314@P10: from a camera (Detect Pose). Given that inserting the gears will require force\nL315@P10-11: sensing, the only robot that can be used is the one that has a force sensor. The 9\nL316@P11: other robot is thus available to move the parts (Transport) from the kit to the work\nL317@P11: area, which may save a little time if it has nothing else to do.\nL318@P11: The gears can be inserted in two different orders. The big gear can be installed\nL319@P11: before the medium-sized gear, or vice versa. It is easier for an automated system\nL320@P11: to install the medium gear first because the alternative requires a sideways motion\nL321@P11: with very tight tolerance and, in any case, cannot be completed if the big gear is\nL322@P11: fully seated. Thus, the sequence is to install the middle gear, followed either by\nL323@P11: the big gear or the small gear.\nL324@P11: There are two options for installing the gears. Either the shafts can be inserted\nL325@P11: into the gears before they are installed, or the shafts can be installed in the base\nL326@P11: and then the gears inserted. In either case, the shaft insertions will be done by a\nL327@P11: person because of the small size of the parts and the need for significant dexterity.\nL328@P11: If the shafts are inserted into the base before the gears are added, there is a\nL329@P11: potential problem because the shafts may slide in their holes and it could be\nL330@P11: difficult for a manipulator to ensure that they stay in place as the force-based\nL331@P11: search is carried out to seat the gear. The alternative of first inserting the shaft into\nL332@P11: the gear and then installing the resulting subassembly is also problematic because\nL333@P11: it is difficult to ensure that the shaft will not slide as it is being installed, making it\nL334@P11: difficult to seat it in the base. A gripper with a third finger that could block the\nL335@P11: shaft from moving is a possible solution. It turns out that the shafts fit very tightly\nL336@P11: into the base so the first option, inserting the shaft in the base is unlikely to cause\nL337@P11: a problem. The hole in the base into which the shaft fits is very short, so locating\nL338@P11: the shaft over it is difficult, which is another reason for a person to carry out this\nL339@P11: operation. Thus, the next step is for the person to pick up the bag of small parts,\nL340@P11: open it, and insert the shafts that will hold the medium gear and the small gear (it\nL341@P11: is more efficient to do them both in the same operation) (Detect Pose, Pick Up,\nL342@P11: Transport, Detect Pose, Align, Insert, Detect Force). While this is being done, the\nL343@P11: second robot can fetch the medium gear (Detect Pose, Pick Up, Transport, Place).\nL344@P11: The medium gear is installed next. This requires the robot with the force sensor to\nL345@P11: pick up the part, align it with the shaft, insert it, and push it down until it is fully\nL346@P11: seated (using force sensing). Since this is the first gear to be inserted, no meshing\nL347@P11: is needed but the base must be held in place using the second robot (Detect Pose,\nL348@P11: Pick Up, Detect Pose, Align, Insert, Detect Force, Retract, Hold)\nL349@P11: Inserting the large gear requires both a force-mediated fit over the shaft on the\nL350@P11: base and meshing with the medium gear. The robot with the force sensor thus\nL351@P11: needs to pick up (Detect Pose, Pick Up) the big gear from where the second robot\nL352@P11: has put it down and carry out an insertion operation (Detect Pose, Align, Insert,\nL353@P11: Detect Force) first to fit the hole in the gear over the shaft and then, while\nL354@P11-12: pushing down, rotate the gear until the teeth mesh (Detect Pose, Insert, Detect 10\nL355@P12: Force, Retract). The second robot must hold (Hold) the base to keep it in place\nL356@P12: during these operations.\nL357@P12: Once the other two gears are installed, the small gear can be installed onto the\nL358@P12: base. This requires picking up (Detect Pose, Pick Up) the gear and moving it\nL359@P12: (Transport) to the assembly area, which is done by the second robot. When\nL360@P12: putting down the part (Place), this robot should turn it over so that it is in the right\nL361@P12: orientation for insertion. The robot with the force sensor then picks up (Detect\nL362@P12: Pose, Pick Up) the component and uses force sensing to assemble it (Detect Pose,\nL363@P12: Align, Insert, Detect Force), while the second robot holds the base (Hold). This\nL364@P12: requires meshing with the teeth of the medium gear while locating the small gear\nL365@P12: over its shaft. This completes the gear subassembly.\nL366@P12: The next step is to install the top cover. This is a difficult operation because all\nL367@P12: the components have to fit into their proper places. It involves a press fit that will\nL368@P12: be completed by the robot and a set of screwing operations that will be done by\nL369@P12: the person. As before, the second robot picks up the part at the kitting area (Detect\nL370@P12: Pose, Pick Up) and moves it to the assembly location (Transport). There, the\nL371@P12: assembly robot picks it up (Detect Pose, Pick Up), moves it to align with the\nL372@P12: assembly (Transport, Detect Pose, Align), and uses force sensing to position it on\nL373@P12: the base (Detect Pose, Place, Detect Force). The positioning has to ensure that the\nL374@P12: top cover is directly over the base, that the shafts of the medium and small gears\nL375@P12: fit into their sockets on the top cover, and that the large gear protrudes from the\nL376@P12: cover. The second robot holds (Hold) the base during this operation. The person\nL377@P12: then inserts the screws while the robot continues to hold the top in place (Detect\nL378@P12: Pose, Insert, Fasten, Detect Force). Then the person and assembly robot move\nL379@P12: away (Retract). The final, completed gearbox is then removed from the assembly\nL380@P12: area and moved to the AGV (Transport). Figure 3 shows the assembled gearbox\nL381@P12: with only the top cover left to attach.\nL382@P12: In practice, it is unlikely that two robots would be used for this task. With only\nL383@P12: one robot, all moves from the kitting area to the assembly area would be carried\nL384@P12: out by that robot as well as the assembly tasks. The biggest change to the\nL385@P12: sequence above would be that a fixture would need to be installed in the assembly\nL386@P12: area into which the base would be placed in the first step. The fixture would hold\nL387@P12: the base still while the rest of the parts were assembled onto it.\nL388@P12: For high-speed, large volume production, a better approach would likely be to\nL389@P12: feed the gears using vibratory mechanisms and to deliver the base and top cover\nL390@P12: on trays or pallets. It would also be helpful to incorporate the gear shafts into the\nL391@P12: extrusion process for the base, which would reduce the need for a person in the\nL392@P12: assembly. Placing the top cover at the end would be done with fixed automation,\nL393@P12-13: and the screws could also be inserted automatically if the top was properly 11\nL394@P13: fixtured, or a different means, such as a snap fit, would be used to attach the top\nL395@P13: cover.\nL396@P13: 3. Determine the required production capacity of the system\nL397@P13: The capacity of the system is not important for this example, but a real system\nL398@P13: would need to be able to assemble the gearboxes in one minute or less to be cost\u0002effective (a person can do it that fast).\nL399@P13: 4. List the feasible assembly techniques for each operation and estimate cost and\nL400@P13: time for each.\nL401@P13: The feasible techniques include manual assembly, automated assembly, and\nL402@P13: mixed assembly. As described above, either manual or mixed assembly is feasible\nL403@P13: for the task. It would be very expensive to implement a completely automated\nL404@P13: assembly station although it would be technically feasible. A fully manual\nL405@P13: assembly would likely be fastest, but a reasonable throughput should be possible\nL406@P13: with mixed assembly.\nL407@P13: 5. Select a set of equipment or people that can make the product at the required rate\nL408@P13: for a reasonable cost.\nL409@P13: This is where the taxonomy is most useful. Given the requirements of the task and\nL410@P13: the available resources (or, if new equipment is to be purchased, the specifications\nL411@P13: of candidate machines), a subset is chosen with the capability to make the\nL412@P13: product. Then, the performance of each piece of equipment is examined to\nL413@P13: determine if it can do so within the required time and cost. To do this, the\nL414@P13: performance of each individual machine is checked using the measures in the\nL415@P13: taxonomy associated with its tasks to be sure that it meets minimal requirements.\nL416@P13: If there are constraints such as limited conditions under which the performance is\nL417@P13: acceptable, a study is required to be sure that the limitations will not adversely\nL418@P13: affect the whole system. Once a piece of equipment is verified to be able to carry\nL419@P13: out parts of the assembly, its performance is checked against any other equipment\nL420@P13: that can perform the same parts and a choice is made based on global performance\nL421@P13: measures such as cost and time. If no machine can perform part of the task\nL422@P13: effectively, adding a person should be considered.\nL423@P13: For the example assembly, there are multiple robots, multiple cameras, an AGV,\nL424@P13: and a person available for the task. As indicated in 2 above, two robots will be\nL425@P13: used. Both have the necessary reach and weight capacity and both are repeatable\nL426@P13: enough and fast enough for their roles. One of the robots has force sensing so it is\nL427@P13: the only one able to perform the gear insertions and meshing. The other robot\nL428@P13: performs pick and place operations and acts as a fixture when parts are being\nL429@P13: added to the base. The actions described above are associated with performance\nL430@P13: measures for each step in the taxonomy. Shneier, et al. [11] contains a number of\nL431@P13: tables that provide metrics for each of the actions as well as properties and\nL432@P13-14: parameters that are important for establishing how well a given piece of 12\nL433@P14: equipment can carry it out. Applying the information in these tables should allow\nL434@P14: equipment to be selected and a sequence of actions to be implemented that meets\nL435@P14: the overall system objectives. There are no guarantees, however, that this will\nL436@P14: always be possible, nor that the resulting system will be optimal for the task.\nL437@P14: The steps in the task require visual sensing to locate the parts and ensure they are\nL438@P14: placed correctly. Two visual sensors are needed, one at the location where the\nL439@P14: parts are picked up from the kit and the other at the assembly station. The sensor\nL440@P14: at the kit must have a large enough field of view to see the whole kit plus a region\nL441@P14: around it in case something is dropped when a part is picked up. It must have\nL442@P14: enough resolution to locate parts to within the grasp uncertainty of the robot and\nL443@P14: its gripper. For picking up parts, the resolution can be lower than for assembly\nL444@P14: because the part will be re-acquired by the other robot. Note that the resolution is\nL445@P14: a function of the camera itself (the size of the pixels on the chip), of the lens, of\nL446@P14: the height of the camera above the work surface, and of the algorithm used to\nL447@P14: locate the parts (typically, algorithms can locate parts to better than 0.1 pixel).\nL448@P14: Thus, there is a lot of flexibility in the selection of a camera.\nL449@P14: The camera used for assembly has similar requirements except that the location\nL450@P14: accuracy must be significantly higher. It is also harder to position the camera to\nL451@P14: be able to see the state of the assembly and still be able to monitor the handover\nL452@P14: of parts from one robot to the other.\nL453@P14: Force sensing is critical to this assembly, being necessary both for the peg-in-hole\nL454@P14: insertion of the gears and for gear meshing. The forces are not expected to be very\nL455@P14: large, however, and commercial force sensors should be able to handle the\nL456@P14: requirements.\nL457@P14: 6. Conduct an economic analysis and proceed to a detailed system design.\nL458@P14: This step is beyond the scope of the paper but Whitney [10] contains a good\nL459@P14: discussion of how to conduct such an analysis.\nL460@P14: 4. Discussion\nL461@P14: Each product presents unique factors to be addressed when determining how best to\nL462@P14: manufacture it. Each manufacturer has slightly different criteria for system performance\nL463@P14: and changing markets further complicate the picture, especially when a long-term\nL464@P14: investment is being considered. Without a careful analysis of the options and their costs\nL465@P14: and benefits, the risk of failure is significantly increased. Lacking measures that can be\nL466@P14: used to compare different options, such an analysis is largely subjective. Thus, the\nL467@P14: availability of performance measures, together with a principled way of applying them, is\nL468@P14: of great value, especially to smaller manufacturers who are unlikely to have the expertise\nL469@P14: and experience to address this complex multi-objective optimization problem.\nL470@P14: The approach described here provides a framework for the analysis although it doesn’t\nL471@P14: supply a mathematical procedure for combining the performance measures of subsystems\nL472@P14: to generate an overall system measure. There are too many unique attributes to each\nL473@P14-15: problem for one method to apply to all. The general framework at least gives an approach 13\nL474@P15: that helps address all the aspects and shows where individual performance measures\nL475@P15: come into play and which aspects of those measures are important.\nL476@P15: As the approach is used, it is likely that gaps will become more obvious both in the\nL477@P15: method itself and in the taxonomy and performance metrics that it uses. This will help\nL478@P15: improve the approach and may also lead to better performance measures for subsystems.\nL479@P15: Some of the methods described in the related work section may be relevant, especially\nL480@P15: simulation, which can be used to explore the candidate solutions and see how well they\nL481@P15: work in the actual factory environment.\nL482@P15: 5. Conclusions\nL483@P15: As robots are applied more frequently to assembly in manufacturing, and as the need for\nL484@P15: quick changes in products and styles increases, there is greater need for ways of\nL485@P15: designing and implementing automated manufacturing workcells that are capable and\nL486@P15: cost effective. Workcells are complex and there are many vendors of components, each\nL487@P15: of which represents a product’s capabilities in a way that emphasizes its best aspects. In\nL488@P15: building an assembly workcell, it is important to be able to compare candidate equipment\nL489@P15: objectively and to have enough information to select the appropriate components. This\nL490@P15: paper has presented an approach based on the work of Whitney and on a taxonomy of\nL491@P15: assembly actions with associated performance measures. It is aimed at enabling small and\nL492@P15: medium-sized manufacturers who lack the resources and in-house expertise to conduct a\nL493@P15: complete analysis each time they need to build a new workcell. Over time, it is expected\nL494@P15: that the performance measures will improve and that the approach will be expanded to\nL495@P15: include more computational methods and guidelines based on experience.\nL496@P15: References\n"
